  General1: You see the time and date, you know exactly what happened, the day has wasted away, but this is no time for excuses so it's time to lock in. WE WILL NOT MOVE until practicum 1 is done and hw3 has been started. Also for class yesterday we did not finish going over the hw but did go over the practicum so I did get the professors examples for that, anyways.
  PRACTICUM 1: WE MADE NO PROGRESS, so this might as well be starting from the beginning anyways so yeah.
	--Problem1: 
  a) make a 300x200 float64 array. Each element containing its own x coordinate, starting with x=0 for column 0 and ending with x = 299 for column 299. How to do this with a loop? First make the array, since the array we are tasked with making is SO large the zeros command is much more efficient( array = np.zeros(rows, columns) ) in this case rows=300 and columns = 200. In order to make sure each row's value matches its index, you can use a for-loop to loop through all the row's of the array( for i in range(array.shape[0]) ) and then set the values within the row's equal to their index( array[i, :] = i ). See code below for EX: 

array = np.zeros((300.0, 200.0))      
for i in range(array.shape[0]):  
    array[i, :] = i              
print(array)

  b) How would you do this without using any loops? Luckily the professor Harrington showed a beautifully elegant way of doing this using broadcasting. First creating a one-dimensional array ( coo = np.arange(300) ) then turning the 1-D array into a 2-D array (coo.shape = (300, 1) ) finally creating a second array with the desired shape using the zero's command( arr = np.zeros((300, 200)) ) and then broadcasting the first array onto the second ( arr+=coo ). See code below for EX:

coo = np.arange(300)
coo.shape = (300, 1)
arr = np.zeros((300,200))
arr+=coo
print(arr)

  c) Display the array using the matplotlib function for showing the image with a map of 'gray'/ WHAT A COINCIDENCE the professor showed us how to do this as well. How fortuitous, see the following line of code for the example. EX. 

put.imshow(arr, cmap = 'gray', origin = 'lower')

imshow displays the given argument(arr in this case) as an image, where each array element is a pixel(which is why the top left of the image is (0,0) because this is also where our array(or matrix is also (0,0), however this is also just the nature of imshow as well ). cmap is the color map(so cmap = 'gray' just makes the image gray). origin = 'lower' just makes it so that instead of the top left corner being the origin, you guessed it it turns the bottom right of the image into the origin(makes row 0 the bottom).

  d) If you are so paranoid you'd like to double check your work and ensure it is 100% up to 'code'(get it), you can do so by simply printing the array or manually choosing and printing random rows within your array. Please see the code below for an EX.

i = 5
